{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.middleware.md | sed -n '20,105p;125,160p;175,196p'\"","aggregated_output":"    20\tis the adapter shape of `datom.world.md` (a map of transform functions)\n    21\tapplied to a handle rather than to a host boundary. Ring's request and\n    22\tresponse pair maps onto the operation map and the contract's outcome map.\n    23\t\n    24\t```clojure\n    25\t{:dao.stream.middleware/in  (fn [ctx req] -> req | outcome-map)\n    26\t :dao.stream.middleware/out (fn [ctx req outcome] -> outcome)}\n    27\t```\n    28\t\n    29\t`in` returning an outcome map short-circuits: the inner handle is not\n    30\tconsulted and that map is the operation's outcome, after the `out`\n    31\ttransforms of the middlewares outside it. `ctx` is a plain map the caller\n    32\tsupplies and no middleware may fabricate; a local caller supplies `{}`.\n    33\t\n    34\t## The operation map\n    35\t\n    36\tAn operation as data. Its vocabulary is `dao.stream.remote.md`'s so that the\n    37\tsame chain runs on either side of a channel without translation:\n    38\t\n    39\t```clojure\n    40\t{:dao.stream.remote/op    :dao.stream/cursor | :dao.stream/next |\n    41\t                          :dao.stream/append!\n    42\t :dao.stream.remote/args  [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n    43\t ...}                     ; open: further qualified keys pass through untouched\n    44\t```\n    45\t\n    46\t`descriptor` and `close!` are not operations a middleware sees: `descriptor`\n    47\tis a name and `close!` is a lifecycle transition, and neither has an outcome\n    48\ta policy may decline (`dao.stream.md`, Close; Envelopes).\n    49\t\n    50\t`(apply-request h ctx req) -> outcome` applies one operation map to a\n    51\thandle. It is the one place an operation map becomes a protocol call.\n    52\t\n    53\t## wrap\n    54\t\n    55\t`(wrap h chain) -> handle`, where `chain` is a vector of middlewares\n    56\tapplied outermost first. The returned handle implements the protocols the\n    57\tinner handle implements and no others. On each protocol call it builds the\n    58\toperation map, runs every `in` from the outermost inward, applies the result\n    59\tto `h` with `apply-request`, and runs every `out` from the innermost\n    60\toutward. Its `descriptor` and `close!` delegate to `h` unchanged.\n    61\t\n    62\tThe wrapped handle is a handle on the same logical stream as `h`: it\n    63\tdeclares `h`'s identity, passes cursors through, and may present values\n    64\tunder its own interpretation (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n    65\t\n    66\t## The position rule\n    67\t\n    68\tA middleware maps position `p` of the inner handle to position `p` of the\n    69\touter handle, for every `p`. It therefore never:\n    70\t\n    71\t- constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or a `gap` recovery\n    72\t  cursor;\n    73\t- changes `:dao.stream/outcome`, `:dao.stream/identity`,\n    74\t  `:dao.stream.remote/op` or `:dao.stream.remote/id`;\n    75\t- skips, merges or reorders elements.\n    76\t\n    77\tIt may replace `(first args)` on `append!` and `:dao.stream/value` on an\n    78\t`ok` from `next` with any value, of any size; add or read open keys; and\n    79\tshort-circuit with an outcome from the operation's exhaustive set. A cipher,\n    80\ta compressor, a redactor and an annotator preserve the rule. A filter that\n    81\tdrops elements does not: it is an interpreter that forwards into a new\n    82\tstream with its own identity (`dao.stream.md`, Composition), never a\n    83\tmiddleware.\n    84\t\n    85\t## The four prohibitions\n    86\t\n    87\tA transform is total and returns at once. It never loops over any handle,\n    88\tnever retries the wrapped operation, never waits, and never touches the\n    89\tinner handle: the only handle operations it may perform are the decision\n    90\tread `gate` defines below (a bounded read on a composed decision medium,\n    91\twhose gap recovery re-reads that medium and whose cursor re-minting after\n    92\ta non-`ok` outcome mints afresh next operation; neither retries the\n    93\twrapped operation) and one `append!` to a\n    94\tside stream it was composed with. It reads no host clock. A transform that\n    95\tneeds facts from a stream is not a middleware: folding a stream is an\n    96\tinterpreter's work, and what a middleware reads is a decision an\n    97\tinterpreter already published onto a medium.\n    98\t\n    99\t## Failure inside a transform\n   100\t\n   101\tA value that cannot be transformed on the way out (undecodable ciphertext,\n   102\ta corrupt compressed value) is presented as\n   103\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/undecodable true :dao.stream.middleware/raw v}`\n   104\tunder `:dao.stream/value`, with the outcome and cursor untouched: the\n   105\tposition is real and the reader decides. A value that cannot be transformed\n   125\tshape `dao.space.query/q` has (a query over immutable inputs, opening\n   126\tnothing), and the required adapter contract for a query interpreter that\n   127\tplugs into the gate (`dao.shibi.md`, How it plugs in).\n   128\t\n   129\t`wrap` calls `cursor` with `:dao.stream/oldest` once when it constructs\n   130\tthe gate. The medium must be open and readable; it has capacity one and\n   131\tevicts its older value. If cursor minting is not `ok`, the gate has no read\n   132\tcursor or decision. Each operation retries `cursor` once; if it is still\n   133\tnot `ok`, `next` is skipped and `verify` receives the none marker. A\n   134\tsuccessful mint is its read cursor.\n   135\t\n   136\tOn each operation, the gate calls `next` once at its read cursor. On `ok`,\n   137\tit stores the successor cursor and value. On `blocked`, it keeps the cursor\n   138\tand last value. On `end`, it clears the value and becomes ended. On `gap`,\n   139\tit clears the value, adopts the recovery cursor, and calls `next` once more.\n   140\tIf that call is `ok`, it stores the successor and value; if `blocked`, it\n   141\tkeeps the recovery cursor without a value; if `end`, it becomes ended; if\n   142\t`gap`, it adopts that recovery cursor and remains without a value. It does\n   143\tnot read a third time in this operation. On any other non-`ok` outcome,\n   144\tit clears the value and cursor, then retries cursor minting next operation.\n   145\t\n   146\t`verify` receives the last value observed, or\n   147\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/none true}` when there is none, or\n   148\t`{:dao.stream.middleware/ended true}` once the gate is ended (the medium\n   149\tclosed; no later operation reads). That value is a bounded snapshot, not a\n   150\tpromise that it is the newest concurrent write.\n   151\t`nil` passes the request through; a reason short-circuits with\n   152\t`{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/refused\n   153\t:dao.stream.middleware/reason reason}`. A policy that must fail closed\n   154\trefuses the marker. The cursor and value are state of the\n   155\twrapped handle, supplied to `wrap`, not global state. The composition must\n   156\tserialize calls on one wrapped handle; the gate has no queue, lock or wait.\n   157\tThe decision medium is not the wrapped stream, so the position rule on the\n   158\tinner handle is untouched.\n   159\t\n   160\tA gate that must account for its own decisions appends each as\n   175\tNeither middleware knows what a credential is. The trivial policy this\n   176\trepository ships with is an allow-list keyed on `:dao.stream.remote/channel`\n   177\tin `ctx`, ignoring the credential. Everything else belongs to\n   178\t[`dao.shibi.md`](./dao.shibi.md).\n   179\t\n   180\t## Exemplars\n   181\t\n   182\tValue encryption: `in` on `:dao.stream/append!` replaces the value with its\n   183\tauthenticated ciphertext; `out` on an `ok` from `:dao.stream/next` replaces\n   184\t`:dao.stream/value` with the plaintext or the undecodable marker. Cursors\n   185\tuntouched. The key is an argument to the constructor that built the\n   186\tmiddleware, exactly as a deposit medium is an argument to a transport\n   187\tconstructor. Ciphers come from libraries.\n   188\t\n   189\tMetering: `out` appends `{:meter/identity id :meter/op op :meter/outcome\n   190\tkind}` to a composed metering stream and returns the outcome unchanged. The\n   191\tside effect is a stream emission (`datom.world.md`, Streams).\n   192\t\n   193\t## Invariant compliance\n   194\t\n   195\tNo callback: a transform is called downward by the caller in the caller's\n   196\tcontrol flow and returns a value; nothing is registered to be invoked\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
